@page "/zhuyin/info"

<PageTitle>Why learn Zhuyin? | HanLearn</PageTitle>

<article class="info-page">
    <header class="info-hero">
        <a class="back-link" href="zhuyin"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Back to Zhuyin</a>
        <p class="eyebrow">The HanLearn approach</p>
        <h1>Why learn Zhuyin?</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            Zhuyin gives Mandarin sounds their own symbols. For many learners, that clean separation makes it easier to listen closely and build new pronunciation habits.
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="history-section" aria-labelledby="history-heading">
        <div class="history-date" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>1913</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>1918</span>
        </div>
        <div>
            <p class="section-label">A short history</p>
            <h2 id="history-heading">Created to annotate Chinese pronunciation</h2>
            <p>
                The first version of Zhuyin was created in the Republic of China in 1913 and officially released in 1918. Its symbols were adapted from simplified forms of Chinese characters and designed to represent the initials, finals, and tones of Mandarin syllables. The familiar name “Bopomofo” comes from its first four symbols: <span lang="zh-Bopo">ㄅ、ㄆ、ㄇ、ㄈ</span>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Zhuyin later became established in Taiwan as a tool for teaching pronunciation and supporting children while they learn to read Chinese characters. It remains part of Taiwanese education, dictionaries, annotated reading, and keyboard input today.
            </p>
            <a class="source-link" href="https://language.moe.gov.tw/001/Upload/files/site_content/M0001/juyin/html_en/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Taiwan Ministry of Education: phonetic-symbol manual <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="reason-grid" aria-label="Reasons for learning Zhuyin">
        <div class="reason-card">
            <span class="reason-number">01</span>
            <h2>Latin letters already carry sound</h2>
            <p>
                Long before studying Mandarin, most learners have spent years connecting Latin letters with the sounds of their own language. Those associations are useful and deeply practised—but they do not always match Mandarin.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
            <span class="reason-number">02</span>
            <h2>Pinyin can invite old habits</h2>
            <p>
                Pinyin uses familiar letters in a precise Chinese spelling system. A learner may still see a familiar letter and instinctively produce the sound or mouth position learned in English or another language. Mandarin initials such as <i>q</i>, <i>x</i>, <i>j</i>, <i>zh</i>, and <i>r</i> are common places where spelling can mislead an untrained ear.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card featured-reason">
            <span class="reason-number">03</span>
            <h2>New symbols create a clean association</h2>
            <p>
                A Zhuyin symbol does not arrive with years of pronunciation baggage. Learning <span lang="zh-Bopo">ㄑ</span> means connecting a new visual symbol directly to a Mandarin sound, rather than first suppressing another language's reading of the letter <i>q</i>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="foundation-section" aria-labelledby="foundation-heading">
        <p class="section-label">Harder first, stronger later</p>
        <h2 id="foundation-heading">Build the sound system before adding the shortcut</h2>
        <div class="foundation-copy">
            <p>
                Pinyin is immediately readable to anyone who already knows the Latin alphabet. That convenience makes Mandarin feel more accessible at the beginning, but recognisable spelling can hide inaccurate pronunciation. Learners may read smoothly while repeatedly using the nearest sound from their first language, allowing habits to become more difficult to notice and correct later.
            </p>
            <p>
                Starting with Zhuyin asks for more effort up front because both the symbols and sounds are new. The payoff is a foundation built around Mandarin pronunciation itself. Once those sound categories are familiar, Pinyin can be learned as a second notation rather than mistaken for instructions to pronounce Mandarin like another Latin-alphabet language.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="learning-path" aria-label="Suggested learning sequence">
            <div>
                <span class="path-step">1</span>
                <strong>Learn the Mandarin sound</strong>
                <span>Connect listening and mouth position to a distinct Zhuyin symbol.</span>
            </div>
            <span class="path-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <div>
                <span class="path-step">2</span>
                <strong>Stabilize the foundation</strong>
                <span>Practise initials, finals, combinations, and tones without relying on familiar spelling.</span>
            </div>
            <span class="path-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <div>
                <span class="path-step">3</span>
                <strong>Add Pinyin</strong>
                <span>Map a useful second notation onto sounds you already recognise.</span>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="qualification">
            Zhuyin cannot remove first-language influence completely, but it can reduce one important source of interference: automatically applying old letter-to-sound rules to a new language.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="accent-section">
        <div class="accent-symbol" lang="zh-Bopo" aria-hidden="true">ㄓ</div>
        <div>
            <p class="section-label">Pronunciation and accent</p>
            <h2>Small sound differences carry a lot of weight</h2>
            <p>
                Native speakers become highly sensitive to the sound patterns of their language while growing up. Differences in consonants, vowels, rhythm, and tones can therefore make a foreign accent immediately noticeable. In Mandarin, some of those differences can also make words harder to understand because sound and tone distinguish meaning.
            </p>
            <p>
                Zhuyin does not guarantee a native-like accent. Careful listening, feedback, and physical pronunciation practice still matter. Its advantage is that it gives those new sounds a notation that is not competing with the spelling habits of another language.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="balance-section">
        <p class="section-label">Why Taiwan uses it</p>
        <h2>Local to Taiwan, useful beyond Taiwan</h2>
        <p>
            Zhuyin is the phonetic system taught in Taiwan, where children use it while learning to read Mandarin. Its limited international use is not a weakness for a Taiwan-focused learner: it also appears in Taiwanese learning materials, dictionaries, keyboards, and annotated texts.
        </p>

        <div class="pinyin-note">
            <h3>Pinyin is still useful</h3>
            <p>
                This is not an argument that Pinyin is bad. Pinyin is an international standard, widely supported, and valuable for typing, dictionaries, and communication with other learners. HanLearn prefers Zhuyin as the first pronunciation map so that Mandarin sounds can be learned on their own terms. Pinyin can be added later without replacing that foundation.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="info-action">
        <div>
            <p class="section-label">Build the association</p>
            <h2>Connect each symbol to its sound.</h2>
        </div>
        <a href="zhuyin">Explore the Zhuyin keyboard <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </footer>
</article>
